WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.001
-Our next guest represents

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:03.536
New York's
14th Congressional District

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.805
in the House of Representatives.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:08.474
Congresswoman
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.276
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:24.457 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:27.026
Well, it is so lovely
to have you here.

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:28.327
-Lovely to be here.
Thank you.

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:30.062
-And it seems like
we planned this,

00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:32.432
but this has been scheduled
for a long time.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.401
No one could have imagined
a couple of months ago

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:37.636
what happened today.
How does this feel?

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:39.972
How are you processing it
as a New Yorker?

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:41.508
-Yeah, well, I mean,

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:44.009
there's as an American
and as a New Yorker.

00:00:44.009 --> 00:00:49.182
As a New Yorker, it was
a very New York kind of day.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.551
You have not just
what was happening

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.253
with former President Trump,

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:56.389
but then you have MTG
flying into town.

00:00:56.389 --> 00:01:00.060
You've got, you know, just
the whole chaos of the scene

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.295
and also all the people
walking by like,

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.430
"Okay. I'm on my lunch break.
It's time to go."

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.932
-Yeah.
-It's -- You know --

00:01:06.932 --> 00:01:09.902
It was -- It was just a lot.
It was a circus, right?

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:12.338
-I mean, your whole life,
this is a person who,

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:16.208
especially in New York,
has been investigated,

00:01:16.208 --> 00:01:18.077
never charged.

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:21.314
Multiple --
certainly accusations

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.349
of financial improprieties.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:25.018
Did you ever think
this day would come

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.453
where he would be arraigned,

00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:29.789
especially for things like,
you know, the misuse of money?

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.791
-Yeah, I mean, I --

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:36.896
I think I had always felt
that something might happen.

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:38.731
Honestly,
I think if something did,

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.833
I would have thought it would be
something like this,

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:45.170
around falsification of business
documents or some sort of charge

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.673
that has to do with
documentation or finances.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:50.109
But, I mean,
my dad was an architect.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:53.613
He worked in construction
and in that industry.

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.214
And from the time
I was a kid,

00:01:55.214 --> 00:01:59.052
it's like no one knew this guy
better than New Yorkers.

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:02.855
And just, like,
this is just --

00:02:02.855 --> 00:02:04.023
People would have thought

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.591
this is what
he would have been in for

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:07.360
before he was president.
-Yeah.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.331
-So, you know, I think it does
feel like a matter of course.

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:13.533
I also think that --
I mean, listen.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:17.103
This is not about guaranteeing
one outcome or another.

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:22.508
This is about due process,
and the lack of process of,

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:25.845
I think, a formal due process
for so long

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:27.580
really created this question of,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.683
do we really have
an equal society,

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:33.052
no matter your income,
no matter how powerful you are?

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:36.622
And I think the presence
of this process demonstrates

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:39.625
that we should have one law
that applies to all people,

00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:41.961
no matter who you are
or where you come from.

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:44.397
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:47.000
-A very true statement.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.868
And also, look.
This is the process.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.536
He's innocent
until proven guilty.

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:52.405
And you were talking
a little backstage

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.773
about how there is oftentimes
a hesitation,

00:02:54.773 --> 00:02:57.810
both in legal circles
and in politics,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:02.448
not to move forward unless
you're certain of the outcome.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.250
-Mm-hmm. Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.719
And I think -- I do. I see this
in a legislative context.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.888
Sometimes you see this
in a legal context,

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:11.024
which is we don't want to try

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:14.060
unless we know what the outcome
is before we start, right?

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.728
We don't want to hold
a vote on something

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:18.697
unless we know for sure
that it's going to pass.

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:20.934
We know that --
You know, the question.

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:23.403
There's been so much legal
speculation over the last week.

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.805
Is this a good idea?
Is it not a good idea?

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:29.442
If we -- If there is evidence
for a crime,

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:32.912
which is what
Alvin Bragg has asserted,

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:34.246
and put before a grand jury

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:36.783
and before a jury
of one's peers,

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:40.286
they themselves move forward
with an indictment,

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:44.157
then this is the process
that must happen in our society.

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:46.459
And then, once again,
there will be another jury

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:48.461
of their peers
to convict or acquit.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:50.129
But we should not make
these decisions

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.499
solely on whether you know
that you're guaranteed

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:54.933
to be successful from the start.

00:03:54.933 --> 00:03:57.469
I think people need
to see these charges

00:03:57.469 --> 00:04:00.573
or see just charges
where there is evidence for it.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:04.610
And I believe that, you know,
the presence of that due process

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.346
is what makes our system fair.

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:09.615
It's a consequence
in and of itself.

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:12.752
And I think it's important.
I just think it's important.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:14.420
-You mentioned Alvin Bragg.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.357
Three GOP House chairs
basically wrote to him

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:20.192
demanding that he come
and testify,

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:24.130
that he bring documents
from this investigation.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.333
It seems like the office hit
back pretty strongly to that.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.969
How do you feel about sort of
the members of Congress

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.171
reaching out and trying
to interfere

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.841
in an investigation like this?
-Yeah, I mean,

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:37.109
I think what they've attempted
to do,

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:40.045
whether it's Jim Jordan

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:42.081
or Chairman Comer
in House Oversight,

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:45.685
where I sit, there needs
to be clear jurisdiction.

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:49.321
And what we have seen from
House Republicans especially

00:04:49.321 --> 00:04:52.324
is that they seem to have
interpreted their role

00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:56.128
as just defending and working
for Donald Trump,

00:04:56.128 --> 00:05:00.065
sometimes more than working
for the kinds of people

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:02.134
that they've been
elected to serve.

00:05:02.134 --> 00:05:05.371
You know, we're seeing
more energy for defending

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:09.241
former President Trump than for
passing gun-safety legislation,

00:05:09.241 --> 00:05:13.579
from helping health care,
from people in, you know --

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.749
from what happened
in the spill in, you know --

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:18.383
I think what we need to see

00:05:18.383 --> 00:05:21.287
is an actual government
that works for people,

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:23.455
no matter what party
you're a part of,

00:05:23.455 --> 00:05:26.459
instead of which partisan person
you're most loyal to.

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:29.629
-I think that's --
They should also know...

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.564
[ Applause ]

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:34.600
When you work for Donald Trump,
you often don't get paid.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.602
And that's something
they should keep in mind.

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:39.905
You questioned Michael Cohen.
This was a few years back.

00:05:39.905 --> 00:05:42.808
This was your first year. You
were a freshman congresswoman.

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:46.078
And you talked to him
about the inflation of assets.

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:48.281
This was a different thing
that Donald Trump did for years.

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:50.950
He would -- Basically, if he was
counting it as an asset,

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:52.752
he would say
it was worth this much.

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:54.987
And if he was paying taxes
on it, he would maybe lower it.

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:57.089
And this actually led --
Tish James has mentioned you

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:58.658
and mentioned that questioning
as leading

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:03.029
to a separate civil case
against Donald Trump.

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:04.930
It's so interesting because
I feel like a lot of times

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.332
you watch people
grandstand in moments

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:08.200
where they get to
question someone.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.003
You laid out -- very simple,
in a way

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:13.138
that people like even I
could understand.

00:06:13.138 --> 00:06:15.040
Do you feel like
people in Congress

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.142
miss the opportunity
to take their time

00:06:17.142 --> 00:06:18.844
and lay something out
very clearly

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:20.679
for the American people
to understand?

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:23.182
-Yeah, you know, I, um --
I actually recently --

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:25.284
So, my first year,

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:28.353
I also held this
kind of Twitter seminar

00:06:28.353 --> 00:06:29.988
for some of my colleagues.

00:06:29.988 --> 00:06:33.426
And this year, I worked with
some of my other colleagues,

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:38.664
including folks like
Maxine Waters and Madeleine Dean

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:40.800
on also kind of teaching folks

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.903
how to use your five minutes
most effectively.

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:45.738
I think, you know --
When we talk about

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:47.473
what your member
of Congress does,

00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:49.041
it's not just legislate.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:50.776
Our job is to investigate.

00:06:50.776 --> 00:06:54.813
And that is a core element
of our duties, to pursue that.

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:57.884
And so I do think --
It takes a lot of preparation.

00:06:57.884 --> 00:06:59.618
It's kind of like
what Mark Twain said.

00:06:59.618 --> 00:07:03.555
"This letter would be shorter,
but I didn't have time."

00:07:03.555 --> 00:07:06.292
That brevity takes an enormous
amount of preparation.

00:07:06.292 --> 00:07:08.261
I kind of look like that scene

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:09.795
from "It's Always Sunny
in Philadelphia"

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:11.063
with the guy like this.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:12.932
-All the yarn connecting...
-All the yarn.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:15.801
That's what my desk looks like
during a hearing sometimes.

00:07:15.801 --> 00:07:19.472
You have to work with
what's happening midstream.

00:07:19.472 --> 00:07:22.240
But I think it's very powerful.
It's really important.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.711
And our job is to educate
and communicate to the public,

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:27.380
in addition to legislating.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:30.750
-I want to talk about other
legislation you're working on.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.885
Before we go to commercial,

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:35.821
Trump was supposedly stuck on
FDR after the arraignment today.

00:07:35.821 --> 00:07:38.424
Is that the most perfect
New York punishment for...?

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:41.460
-Okay.
I have to take issue

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.062
because I represent
LaGuardia Airport.

00:07:44.062 --> 00:07:46.032
-I told you. It's nice now.
-Okay?

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:47.533
-I told you. It's nice now.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:49.234
-We put a lot of work into that.

00:07:49.234 --> 00:07:50.970
-And I'm telling you.
It is great.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.839
I'm a huge fan of LaGuardia now.

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:56.007
It's just hard for us as comedy
writers. You took someth--

00:07:56.007 --> 00:07:57.843
This would be like if
the Mets won the World Series.

00:07:57.843 --> 00:07:59.712
-I know.
-What would we do?

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:01.047
-I know.
What would we joke about?

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:03.849
What I will say is
that I do think it was an omen

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:06.385
that he landed
in my congressional district.

00:08:06.385 --> 00:08:08.620
-[ Laughs ] Yeah. I think --
-Show him who's boss!

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.189
-There you go.

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:12.390
We'll be right back
with more from Congresswoman

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.193
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

